    "text": "Mobility of electrons in a semiconductor is defined as the ratio of their drift velocity to the applied electric field. If, for an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>N</mtext></math>-type semiconductor, the density of electrons is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>19</mn></msup><mi>&#160;</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math> and their mobility is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1.6</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, then the resistivity of the semiconductor (since it is an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>N</mtext></math>-type semiconductor contribution of holes is ignored) is close to:",
